<p class="prose">Last updated: <time datetime="{{ dateModified }}">{{ dateModified }}</time></p>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="target">
  <h2 id="target">The standard we aim for</h2>
  <p>
    The target is
    <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/" rel="noopener">WCAG 2.2</a>
    level AA. It is a target, not a result: <strong>this site does not
    claim to conform to WCAG 2.2 AA</strong>, or to any other level, and
    no one has audited it against the full standard.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="measured">
  <h2 id="measured">What is checked automatically</h2>
  <p>
    Three automated checks run in continuous integration on the built site.
    Each is limited, and the limits are listed with it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Colour contrast, in both themes.</strong> The
      <a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core" rel="noopener">axe-core</a>
      <code>color-contrast</code> rule runs in the light theme and in the
      dark theme on every page in the sitemap, plus the unlisted dashboard,
      style guide, Mermaid sandbox and newsletter pages. It also runs on
      the Change Feed Playground in several states (onboarding, feed after
      seeding, compare, and the three guided labs), in both themes. The
      list of excused elements is empty. Text drawn over a gradient is
      something axe cannot score; for the large gradient headlines the
      project measured the ratios separately, once, with a script.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A wider axe rule set on six pages.</strong> The home page,
      overview, introduction, quickstarts, tooling and troubleshooting are
      checked against axe's WCAG 2.0 and 2.1 A and AA rules. Only
      violations that axe rates critical or serious fail the build. On four
      of those pages some rules are deliberately filtered out as known
      issues (see below). Diagrams drawn by Mermaid are excluded.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>pa11y with the WCAG 2 AA ruleset</strong> on eight built
      pages, a separate engine from axe.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What automated checks cannot catch.</strong> Tools like these
    find only a minority of accessibility problems. They cannot tell
    whether alt text or link text makes sense, whether the reading order
    and focus order are sensible, whether a diagram or an interactive
    demo can be understood or operated without a mouse or without sight,
    or whether the content is clear. The axe rule sets used are tagged for
    WCAG 2.0 and 2.1, not for the criteria that are new in 2.2, so those
    are not checked automatically. The only keyboard test in the suite
    checks that the theme toggle can take focus.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="not-tested">
  <h2 id="not-tested">What has not been done</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Testing with assistive technology has not yet been done.</strong>
    No one has recorded a pass with a screen reader, a switch device or
    voice control, and there is no recorded keyboard-only or 200 percent
    zoom review. A test with real assistive technology on the home page,
    the introduction, a module page, the playground and the assistant
    panel is planned but has not happened. No disabled users have been
    consulted.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="limits">
  <h2 id="limits">Known limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Filtered rules on four pages.</strong> The automated suite
      ignores some axe rules there because the issues pre-date the check:
      <code>/intro/</code> (<code>aria-prohibited-attr</code>,
      <code>svg-img-alt</code>, <code>label</code>,
      <code>aria-allowed-attr</code>), <code>/quickstarts/</code>
      (<code>label</code>, <code>link-name</code>,
      <code>aria-allowed-attr</code>), <code>/tooling/</code>
      (<code>aria-allowed-attr</code>) and <code>/troubleshooting/</code>
      (<code>label</code>). These may be real barriers, for example a
      form field without a label or a link without a name, and they are
      not fixed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Interactive demos and diagrams.</strong> The event demo, the
      Change Feed Playground and the Mermaid diagrams are checked for
      colour contrast at most. Whether they can be operated by keyboard,
      or understood with a screen reader, has not been tested, and the
      assistant panel has no automated accessibility check beyond the
      page-level ones. Treat them as likely to have barriers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Third-party content.</strong> Some pages load libraries or
      embeds from other hosts, described on the
      <a href="{{ '/privacy/' | url }}">privacy page</a>. Their
      accessibility is outside this project's control.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="feedback">
  <h2 id="feedback">Report a barrier</h2>
  <p>
    If something on the site is hard or impossible to use, please say what
    page, what you were trying to do, and what got in the way. Your
    assistive technology and browser help if you want to share them.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Write to the maintainer at the public address on the
      <a href="{{ '/contact/' | url }}#general">contact page</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Or open a public issue with the
      <a href="https://github.com/{{ site.repository }}/issues/new?template=bug.yml">bug form</a>
      on GitHub. Issues are public, so do not put anything private in one.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is a one-person project, so there is no promised reply time and
    no promised fix date.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="ai-assisted">
  <h2 id="ai-assisted">How this statement was written</h2>
  <p>
    This page was drafted with AI assistance from the repository's test
